{{- /*
The two halves of the adoption guard (ARCHITECTURE.md 7.4).

Section 11.7's rule is that two servers must never run against one world, and
during an adoption week the box has two units that could. A Conflicts= in each
direction makes the SERVICE MANAGER refuse it, which holds when gvmd is dead,
when the operator types `systemctl start zomboid` out of habit, and when a
forgotten `enable` fires the legacy unit at the next reboot.

These are 20-*.conf, not part of the 10-gvm.conf `gvm apply` rewrites every
run: they are written once by `gvm adopt cutover` and removed once by `gvm
adopt finalize` (section 7.1's numbering contract). They also could not live in
the generated drop-in even if the numbering allowed it, because the legacy
unit's name is not in the instance YAML -- only the cutover knows it.
*/ -}}
{{- define "adopt-guard" -}}
# GENERATED BY `gvm apply` -- DO NOT EDIT.
# Written by `gvm adopt cutover {{ .Instance }}`; removed by `gvm adopt finalize {{ .Instance }}`.
# The reciprocal lives in the legacy unit's own drop-in directory.
[Unit]
# systemd itself refuses to run {{ .LegacyUnit }} and this unit against the same
# world. After= orders the two so a cutover that stops one and starts the other
# does not race.
Conflicts={{ .LegacyUnit }}
After={{ .LegacyUnit }}
{{ end -}}

{{- define "legacy-guard" -}}
# GENERATED BY `gvm apply` -- DO NOT EDIT.
# Written by `gvm adopt cutover {{ .Instance }}`; removed by `gvm adopt finalize {{ .Instance }}`.
# This is a gvm-owned drop-in on a unit gvm does not own. It adds one directive
# and changes nothing else about {{ .LegacyUnit }}, so removing this file
# restores that unit exactly as it was.
[Unit]
Conflicts={{ .Unit }}
{{ end -}}
